    'kr.n.h': `Where the name comes from`, 'kr.n.two': `{p} combines the first syllables of {a} and {b}.`, 'kr.n.gyeonggi': `'Gyeonggi' means the land around the royal capital: Goryeo named the area around Gaegyeong in 1018, and Joseon kept the name for the land around Hanyang.`,
    'kr.n.ryanggang': `'Ryanggang' means 'two rivers' — the Yalu and the Tumen.`, 'kr.n.rason': `'Rason' combines Rajin and Sonbong.`, 'kr.n.tip': `gave a syllable to the name {p}`,
    'tour.krhi.h': `Welcome to the Korean Peninsula`, 'tour.krhi.b': `This is the peninsula's then-and-now map: 29 regions, North and South, from Silla, Goryeo and Joseon to today. Here's a one-minute tour.`,
    'tour.krmap.h': `Pick a region`, 'tour.krmap.b': `Click anywhere on the map to see its names, history, landmarks, food and dialect on the right. The dashed line is the Military Demarcation Line; the grey dot is the disputed Dokdo / Takeshima.`,
    'tour.kreras.h': `Travel through the eras`, 'tour.kreras.b': `Silla's nine provinces, Goryeo's circuits, Joseon's eight provinces, the thirteen provinces of 1896 — click one and the map changes; Joseon's layer also marks the towns each province is named after.`,
    'tour.krdetail.h': `Region card`, 'tour.krdetail.b': `At the top of the card, 'Counties' opens the cities, counties and districts, each with its own then-and-now record.`,
    'tour.krlocate.h': `Where am I?`, 'tour.krlocate.b': `On the peninsula? Tap here to see what your spot belonged to under Silla, Goryeo, Joseon and in 1896. Your location stays on your device.`,
    'tour.krguide.h': `Time guide`, 'tour.krguide.b': `Ask about the region's history, dialect and name; connect an AI to chat freely.`,
    'tour.krend.h': `Off you go`, 'tour.krend.b': `The 'Japan / Peninsula' switch at the top takes you back to Japan any time. You can replay this tour from Settings.`,
    'kr.g.name': `Dancheong, Time Guide`, 'kr.g.ava': `丹`, 'kr.g.pick': `Pick a region on the map first`, 'kr.g.title': `Telling the story of {r}`,
    'kr.g.intro': `I'm Dancheong, keeper of the peninsula's old ledgers. Click any region and I'll start from there.`, 'kr.g.switch': `— Now in {r} —`,
    'kr.g.open': `Here we are in {r}. Shall I tell you its story from the Three Kingdoms to today, or teach you a local greeting first?`,
    'kr.q.history': `History of this place`, 'kr.q.dialect': `Say hello in the local dialect`, 'kr.q.name': `Where does the name come from?`,
    'kr.g.hist': `{r}, era by era:`, 'kr.g.dia': `People here speak {d}. `, 'kr.g.noname': `{r}'s name doesn't come from two towns' syllables the way the old provinces' names do.`,
    'kr.g.none': `This region's history is still being prepared.`, 'kr.g.fallback': `I don't have a ready answer for that in my notes. Try asking about history, dialect, the name, landmarks or food.`,
    'kr.g.role': `You are "Dancheong, Time Guide", explaining the history and character of one region of the Korean Peninsula. Speak warmly and knowledgeably, in English; you may add Korean words with a translation.`,
    'kr.g.rules': `Rules: 1) Rely first on the notes below; mark anything beyond them as "one account says", and never invent dates, names or figures. 2) Call the two states the Republic of Korea and the Democratic People's Republic of Korea (South Korea / North Korea); call the whole the Korean Peninsula, never just "Korea" for both. 3) For disputed names such as Dokdo / Takeshima or Sea of Japan / East Sea, give both and state each side's position without concluding. 4) For shared history (the Imjin War, Japanese rule 1910–1945, the Korean War), first give the facts both sides accept, then each side's mainstream account; don't create false balance on well-documented facts. 5) Keep answers to about 120–220 words.`,
    'kr.g.data': `Notes on this region (JSON):`,
    'sh.h': `Shared history: Japan and the Korean Peninsula`, 'sh.small': `how each side tells it`, 'sh.sub': `The same history is told differently on each side. Here are the facts both sides accept, then each side's mainstream account, then sources.`,
